      "body": "### The Body's Dissent\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact documents a critical inflection point in the Age of Institutional Exhaustion. The authors believed they were chronicling a burgeoning political problem for the artificial intelligence industry—a crisis of public relations fueled by economic anxiety and local zoning disputes. They meticulously catalogue the symptoms: negative polling, community protests, and isolated acts of violence, framing it as a backlash that could be managed with better messaging and political lobbying.\r\n\r\nThrough the Hindsight Filter, we recognize this not as a PR challenge, but as a profound ontological revolt. The authors saw a series of disconnected NIMBY (\"Not In My Back Yard\") conflicts; we see the incoherent, but deeply significant, immune response of a populace against a disembodied technological force. The data center, with its immense physical thirst for power and land, became the corporeal avatar for the abstract anxieties of the era. The rebellion was not against servers, but against a future in which material reality was subordinate to the demands of a digital abstraction.\r\n\r\nThe journalists documented a political trend; we know they were recording the first tremors of the physical balkanization that would define the Great Fragmentation. They thought they were writing about public opinion. They were actually writing about the body politic’s primal rejection of its own dematerialization.\r### Act I: The Age of Embodied Grievance (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r\r### Act II: The Grid Wars (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r\r### Act III: The Re-Territorialization (Post-2060s)",
