      "preview": "The Leviathan Learns to Hash This artifact documents a pivotal, yet misunderstood, moment in the Age of Institutional Exhaustion. The actors involved believed they were witnessing the integration of a novel \"computer science tool\" into the national security apparatus of a legacy nation-state. Adm...",
      "body": "### The Leviathan Learns to Hash\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact documents a pivotal, yet misunderstood, moment in the Age of Institutional Exhaustion. The actors involved believed they were witnessing the integration of a novel \"computer science tool\" into the national security apparatus of a legacy nation-state. Admiral Paparo’s careful distinction—that his interest in Bitcoin was for cryptography and network security, not its financial properties—was an attempt to intellectually domesticate a feral technology. He sought to harness the protocol’s security features for the projection of state power, assuming the American empire could extract the utility of Proof-of-Work without inheriting its philosophical commitments to decentralization.\r\n\r\nThrough the Hindsight Filter, we see this exchange not as the state mastering a new tool, but as the first instance of a decentralized protocol imposing its logic onto a centralized institution. The participants thought they were discussing a tactical asset for enhancing existing command and control systems. They were actually documenting the initial stages of Protocol Co-option, a process that would ultimately invert the relationship between the state and the network. The U.S. military was not simply running a node; it was unwittingly seeding its own operational structure with a logic that would contribute to the Great Fragmentation. They believed they were sharpening a sword; we know they were forging their own chains.\r### Act I: The Phase of Protocol Co-option (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r### Act II: The Kinetic Fragmentation Phase (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r### Act III: The Age of Cryptographic Sovereignty (Post-2060s)",
