      "preview": "The State's First Sip of the Protocol Sea The Summary: This artifact documents a critical inflection point in the Age of Institutional Exhaustion: the moment a 20th-century sovereign power first attempted to formally integrate a decentralized, non-sovereign protocol into its national security app...",
      "body": "### The State's First Sip of the Protocol Sea\r\n\r\n\r\n**The Summary:** This artifact documents a critical inflection point in the Age of Institutional Exhaustion: the moment a 20th-century sovereign power first attempted to formally integrate a decentralized, non-sovereign protocol into its national security apparatus. The exchange between Congressman Gooden and Admiral Paparo is revealing. They believed they were discussing the acquisition of a new \"computer science tool\"—a novel method of cryptography to \"secure networks and project power.\" They frame the Bitcoin protocol as an instrument, an inert object to be wielded in geopolitical competition, akin to a new cryptographic standard or another strategic resource like oil.\r\n\r\nThrough the Hindsight Filter, we see their profound conceptual error. They were not acquiring a tool; they were interfacing with an alternative logic of power. They thought they could use the protocol’s properties—immutable time-stamping, decentralized verification—to reinforce the centralized nation-state. What they were actually documenting was the state’s first, tentative step toward sanctioning its own eventual irrelevance in matters of trust and settlement. They believed they were experimenting with a new weapon for the old game, failing to realize the protocol itself was a new game entirely.\r### Act I: The Co-option Doctrine (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r### Act II: The Protocol Wars (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r### Act III: The Age of Bounded Trust (Post-2060s)",
