      "preview": "The Party at the End of the World This artifact is a searing piece of cultural criticism. It is a work of moral history, written by a man who has looked at the loneliness and sterility of his era and correctly identified it not as a recession, but as a hangover. He sees a generation that was sold...",
      "body": "# The Party at the End of the World\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact is a searing piece of cultural criticism. It is a work of moral history, written by a man who has looked at the loneliness and sterility of his era and correctly identified it not as a recession, but as a hangover. He sees a generation that was sold a bill of goods—a \"free for all\" of limitless, frictionless pleasure—and is now waking up in a cold, empty bed, wondering where the future went.\r\nFrom my perspective in 2100, we read this as one of the most important diagnostic texts of [[The Sterile Plague]]. The author believes he is describing a temporary dip in activity. We see him describing the onset of the Great Abdication, the moment a civilization decided, en masse, to stop reproducing itself because the effort required to connect with another human being had become greater than the reward.\r\nAllow me to trace the full, devastating history of this hangover, from the empty bedrooms of your time to the full nurseries of mine.\r\nHere is the constellation of this history, viewed through the Hindsight Filter:\r### Act I: The Perfect Trap (2010s-2030s)\r\r\r\r\r### Act II: [[The Great Unpairing]] (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r\r### Act III: The Return of the Sacred (Post-2060s)",
