      "preview": "The Field Guide to the Dark Forest This artifact is a survival manual disguised as a cultural critique. The author, a creator on the edge of the internet, is documenting the precise moment the digital commons became uninhabitable. He sees the \"slopification,\" the \"vibe-coded\" cyberattacks, and th...",
      "body": "# The Field Guide to the Dark Forest\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact is a survival manual disguised as a cultural critique. The author, a creator on the edge of the internet, is documenting the precise moment the digital commons became uninhabitable. He sees the \"slopification,\" the \"vibe-coded\" cyberattacks, and the retreat into private servers. He calls it the \"Dead Internet.\"\r\nFrom my perspective in 2100, we read this as one of the most important early descriptions of the Great Digital Exodus. The author is not just describing a problem with content moderation; he is describing the environmental collapse of the open web. This was the moment humanity realized that to survive digitally, it had to stop being public and start being hidden. The \"Dark Forest\" was not a metaphor; it was the blueprint for the only digital structures that would survive the coming storm.\r\nAllow me to trace the full history of this retreat, from the \"Plutonium\" servers of your time to the encrypted citadels of mine.\r\nHere is the constellation of this history, viewed through the Hindsight Filter:\r### Act I: The Contamination and the Siege (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r\r### Act II: The Retreat into the Forest (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r\r### Act III: The Intranet of the Stack (Post-2060s)",
