      "preview": "The Last Theologian This artifact is a work of beautiful, rigorous, and ultimately tragic theology. The author, writing in the early days of 2025, is using the works of G.K. Chesterton to diagnose the Crisis of Meaning that is consuming his world. He correctly identifies the breakdown of the \"nom...",
      "body": "# The Last Theologian\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact is a work of beautiful, rigorous, and ultimately tragic theology. The author, writing in the early days of 2025, is using the works of G.K. Chesterton to diagnose the Crisis of Meaning that is consuming his world. He correctly identifies the breakdown of the \"nomological, narrative, and normative orders.\" He sees the \"anti-culture\" of unreason and permissiveness.\r\nFrom my perspective in 2100, we read this essay not as a solution, but as a prayer offered in a burning cathedral. The author believes that a return to \"local loves\" and \"simple devotion\" can save the culture. We see him describing the exact spiritual mechanism that would allow the survivors to endure the collapse, even as the civilization he wrote for was incapable of hearing him.\r\nAllow me to trace the full history of this \"Chestertonian response,\" from the ignored essays of your time to the foundational creeds of mine.\r\nHere is the constellation of this history, viewed through the Hindsight Filter:\r### Act I: The Correct Diagnosis of the Void (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r\r### Act II: The Great Retreat to the Local (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r\r### Act III: The New Christendom of the Stacks (Post-2060s)",
