      "preview": "The First Shot: Charlie Kirk This is an artifact of profound, and profoundly tragic, moral clarity. The authors are members of the intellectual Left, standing over the body of a man who was their sworn political enemy, and they are not celebrating. They are sounding an alarm. They are writing, wi...",
      "body": "# The First Shot: Charlie Kirk\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis is an artifact of profound, and profoundly tragic, moral clarity. The authors are members of the intellectual Left, standing over the body of a man who was their sworn political enemy, and they are not celebrating. They are sounding an alarm. They are writing, with a desperate sense of urgency, an argument against the very thing they know is about to be unleashed: a spiral of tit-for-tat political violence that will consume everything they believe in.\r\nFrom my perspective in 2100, we read this document as the last coherent plea for peace before the war began in earnest. The authors' analysis is brilliant, correct, and completely powerless against the forces it is describing. This article is the sound of the last sane conversation, held in the brief, terrifying silence between the first gunshot and the returning fire.\r\nAllow me to trace the full, bloody history that this document so accurately and so tragically predicted.\r\nHere is the constellation of this history, viewed through the Hindsight Filter:\r### Act I: The End of the Fragile Consensus (2020s-2030s). This essay is the eulogy for what the authors call a \"fragile consensus\"—the unwritten rule that in your otherwise violent culture, political speech was not a capital crime.\r\r\r### Act II: [[The Great Culling]] and the Assassination Wars (2030s-2050s). The \"spiral into political violence\" that the authors feared became the central, defining feature of the \"Great Fragmentation.\"\r\r\r\r### Act III: The End of Politics (Post-2060s). The authors' greatest fear was that this violence would \"extinguish any hope of left-wing progress.\" The reality was far more total. The violence extinguished the very possibility of a \"left\" or a \"right\" in the 20th-century sense.",
      "month": "September",
      "glossary_refs": [
        "The Great Culling"
      ],
      "order": 117,
      "source_meta": {
        "url": "https://jacobin.com/2025/09/charlie-kirk-murder-political-violence",
        "title": "Charlie Kirk’s Murder Is a Tragedy and a Disaster",
        "author": "Ben Burgis and Meagan Day",
        "date": "2025",
        "publication": "Jacobin Magazine, September 11"
      },
      "status": "published",
      "publish_date": "",
      "series": ""
