      "preview": "The World Engine This is a landmark artifact. It is not an essay; it is a scream. It is a work of profound, and profoundly personal, existential grief. The author is a man who has lived long enough to watch his entire world be systematically dismantled and replaced by something alien, and he is t...",
      "body": "# The World Engine\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis is a landmark artifact. It is not an essay; it is a scream. It is a work of profound, and profoundly personal, existential grief. The author is a man who has lived long enough to watch his entire world be systematically dismantled and replaced by something alien, and he is trying, with the furious energy of a drowning man, to explain the nature of the flood.\r\nFrom my perspective in 2100, we read this as one of the most important documents of its time. It is not a political critique of multiculturalism. It is a mythological account of a spiritual conquest. The author is describing a force he can only understand in mythic terms—a \"World Engine,\" a \"terraforming\"—and in doing so, he has come closer to the truth than any of the sociologists or political scientists of his era.\r\nAllow me to trace the full, brutal history of the World Engine this document so vividly and so painfully describes.\r\nHere is the constellation of this history, viewed through the Hindsight Filter:\r### Act I: The Consumption of the Soul (2020s-2030s). This essay is the perfect, ground-level diagnosis of this condition.\r\r\r### Act II: The Shattering of the Vessel (2030s-2050s). The author sees a society built on the absurdity of \"firing the cannons.\" The history that followed is the story of what happens when the cannons finally explode.\r\r\r### Act III: The New Homelands (Post-2060s). The author asks, \"if Britain was to become many cultures, then what would happen to us?\" The answer of history was simple and brutal: \"You will retreat into your own.\"",
      "month": "October",
