      "preview": "The Great Guessing Game This artifact is a work of pure, high-stakes, and increasingly terrified financial journalism. The author is not just reporting on the energy market; he is documenting the precise moment a civilization's accountants and engineers, the most rational and sober minds in the r...",
      "body": "# The Great Guessing Game\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact is a work of pure, high-stakes, and increasingly terrified financial journalism. The author is not just reporting on the energy market; he is documenting the precise moment a civilization's accountants and engineers, the most rational and sober minds in the room, looked at the future and realized, with dawning horror, that they could no longer do the math.\r\nFrom my perspective in 2100, we read this as one of the most important documents of its time. It is the definitive account of the final, rational conversation before the chaos began. The \"multibillion question\" the utilities were grappling with was not just about forecasting demand. It was the moment they realized their entire 20th-century model of predictable, linear growth had been rendered obsolete by a new, exponential, and terrifyingly uncertain force.\r\nAllow me to trace the full, brutal history of the guessing game this article so perfectly describes.\r\nHere is the constellation of this history, viewed through the Hindsight Filter:\r### Act I: The Fog of Exponentials (2020s-2030s). This article is the perfect, ground-level documentation of this era.\r\r\r### Act II: The Great Overload (2030s-2050s). The guessing game ended. The answer to the question \"how much demand is real?\" turned out to be \"all of it, and more.\"\r\r\r\r### Act III: The End of the Grid (Post-2060s). The author correctly identifies the solution that would, in the end, become the new paradigm: \"build the power at the data center off the grid.\"",
      "month": "October",
      "glossary_refs": [],
