      "preview": "The Last Generation of the Old World This artifact is a work of profound and elegant melancholy. It is a long, contemplative essay from the heart of the 20th-century intellectual tradition, wrestling with the slow-motion apocalypse of depopulation. The author, David Runciman, is staring into the ...",
      "body": "# The Last Generation of the Old World\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact is a work of profound and elegant melancholy. It is a long, contemplative essay from the heart of the 20th-century intellectual tradition, wrestling with the slow-motion apocalypse of depopulation. The author, David Runciman, is staring into the demographic abyss and finding no way out. He sees the \"oppressive\" future of an inverted family tree, the futility of policy tweaks, and the deep, structural link between modernity and sterility.\r\nFrom my perspective in 2100, we read this not as a question (\"Are we doomed?\"), but as a definitive answer. The author has correctly identified the terminal condition of [[The Crisis of Posterity]]. He has seen that the modern, liberal, individualistic world is biologically self-terminating. His only error was in assuming that \"humanity\" would die with it.\r\nAllow me to trace the full history of the \"doom\" this essay contemplates, and the strange, new life that emerged from it.\r\nHere is the constellation of this history, viewed through the Hindsight Filter:\r### Act I: The Correct Diagnosis of a Dying Species (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r\r### Act II: [[The Great Culling]] by Nature (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r\r### Act III: The New Speciation (Post-2060s)",
      "month": "December",
      "glossary_refs": [
        "The Crisis of Posterity",
        "The Great Culling"
      ],
      "order": 204,
      "source_meta": {
        "url": "https://archive.is/r8ZP5",
        "title": "Are we doomed?",
        "author": "David Runciman",
        "date": "Vol. 47 No. 21 · 20 November 2025",
        "publication": "London Review of Books"
      },
      "status": "published",
      "publish_date": "",
      "series": ""
