      "preview": "The Brittle Monopoly This artifact documents a minor bureaucratic skirmish that, through the Hindsight Filter, reveals a foundational stress fracture in the edifice of the 20th-century centralized state. The authors believed they were chronicling a policy debate about \"school choice,\" framing it ...",
      "body": "### The Brittle Monopoly\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact documents a minor bureaucratic skirmish that, through the Hindsight Filter, reveals a foundational stress fracture in the edifice of the 20th-century centralized state. The authors believed they were chronicling a policy debate about \"school choice,\" framing it as a conflict between student opportunity and institutional intransigence. They correctly identified the symptoms—declining academic performance, parental frustration, ideological rigidity—but mistook them for the disease itself. The disease was Institutional Exhaustion.\r\n\r\nThe Michigan State Board of Education’s reflexive opposition to a federally incentivized scholarship program was not, as the authors suggest, merely a poor political choice. It was the terminal logic of a system built for a world that no longer existed. The board was not simply choosing ideology over students; it was acting as any legacy institution does in its final phase: prioritizing self-preservation above its stated mission. They thought they were debating the merits of a pilot program. We know they were documenting the first tremors of the system's complete structural failure, mistaking the shudder of a collapsing pillar for an argument in a committee room.\r### Act I: The Age of Curricular Secession (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r\r### Act II: The Great Unbundling (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r\r### Act III: The Era of Sovereign Stacks (Post-2060s)",
