      "preview": "The Calculus of Grievance The author of this artifact, William Briggs, correctly identified a key pathology of the Age of Institutional Exhaustion: the symbiotic relationship between a credentialed \"Expertocracy\" and the social technology of \"Official Victims.\" He believed he was critiquing a pol...",
      "body": "### The Calculus of Grievance\r\n\r\n\r\nThe author of this artifact, William Briggs, correctly identified a key pathology of the Age of Institutional Exhaustion: the symbiotic relationship between a credentialed \"Expertocracy\" and the social technology of \"Official Victims.\" He believed he was critiquing a political power-grab, a cynical mechanism used by failing elites to maintain control. He meticulously documents how any \"calamity,\" most notably the pervasive climate anxieties of the era, could be used as a substrate to cultivate and harvest grievances from an ever-expanding taxonomy of social groups.\r\n\r\nThrough the Hindsight Filter, we see this phenomenon not as a strategy for control, but as a systemic death spiral. Briggs saw a flaw in the machine; we see the machine consuming its own gears. He was documenting the final, inflationary stage of a social order based on centralized arbitration of status. The endless manufacturing of \"marginal groups\" was not a sign of the system's strength, but of its terminal desperation. It was attempting to solve its legitimacy crisis by minting a currency of grievance, devaluing the very concept of social cohesion until it became worthless and paved the way for the Great Fragmentation.\r### Act I: The Grievance Economy (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r### Act II: The Collapse of Adjudication (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r### Act III: The Flight to Proof (Post-2060s)",
