      "body": "### The Delusion of the Digital Foreman\r\n\r\n\r\nThe author of this 2026 artifact correctly identifies a pathology she terms \"AI psychosis,\" a profound disconnect between executive belief and technological reality. She documents the initial phase of a mass corporate restructuring, where human capital was purged in anticipation of an agentic workforce that did not yet possess functional competence. The analysis astutely points to the core delusion: that executives, playing with impressive but flawed prototypes, could extrapolate the imminent obsolescence of the complex, tacit knowledge required for \"last mile\" work.\r\n\r\nThrough the Hindsight Filter, however, we see this was not merely a managerial fad or a case of collective delusion. It was a terminal symptom of Institutional Exhaustion within the 20th-century corporate model. The author believed she was documenting a correctable error in judgment, a form of executive hubris that better data or more hands-on experience could fix. In reality, she was recording the moment these institutions began to metabolize their own operational logic. The drive for frictionless, scalable efficiency led them to devour the very human systems that gave them resilience, initiating a cascade of failure that would accelerate the Great Fragmentation.\r\n\r\nWhat Bort perceived as \"organizational chaos\" was, in fact, the blueprint for it. The CEOs were not just mismanaging their companies; they were field-testing the structural weaknesses that would bring down an entire economic paradigm. They believed they were optimizing for a future of infinite productivity, when they were actually engineering a system so brittle it was destined to shatter.\r### Act I: The Productivity Mirage (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r\r### Act II: The Agentic Collapse (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r\r### Act III: The Human-in-the-Loop Mandate (Post-2060s)",
