      "preview": "The Great Unraveling of Flow This artifact is a clinical accounting of a system entering cardiac arrest. The authors, operating within the intellectual confines of the Age of Institutional Exhaustion, believed they were documenting a severe but manageable energy crisis—a logistical problem of inv...",
      "body": "### The Great Unraveling of Flow\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact is a clinical accounting of a system entering cardiac arrest. The authors, operating within the intellectual confines of the Age of Institutional Exhaustion, believed they were documenting a severe but manageable energy crisis—a logistical problem of inventories, price spikes, and potential recession. They meticulously chart the drawdown of the global oil buffer, using metaphors like a \"shock absorber\" to describe the function of strategic reserves. They saw the 2026 Iran War as the cause of the shock, a geopolitical event stressing an otherwise functional system.\r\n\r\nThrough the Hindsight Filter, we see this document not as an analysis of a temporary supply disruption, but as a final, fatal diagnosis of the Globalized Hydrocarbon Economy itself. The authors were not observing a system being tested; they were recording the moment its fundamental design flaws became terminal. The \"shock absorber\" was not just compressing; it was shattering. The war was merely the precipitating event that revealed the system's absolute fragility, an architecture built upon the naive assumption of perpetual, frictionless flow through a handful of indefensible chokepoints.\r\n\r\nWhat they mistook for a market problem to be solved by price signals and government releases was, in fact, a thermodynamic crisis. The world was reaching its physical limit. The authors worried about the cost of the next barrel; history notes that the true crisis began when there was no next barrel to be had, at any price.\r### Act I: The Thermodynamic Limit (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r\r### Act II: The Scramble for Sovereignty (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r\r### Act III: The Age of Autarky (Post-2060s)",
