      "preview": "Three Acres and a Fabricator This artifact documents a nascent ideology, \"neo-distributism,\" which the author presents as a voluntary social and cultural movement for the 2020s. He frames it as an optimistic choice, a \"third way\" enabled by new domestic fabrication technologies that could allow c...",
      "body": "### Three Acres and a Fabricator\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact documents a nascent ideology, \"neo-distributism,\" which the author presents as a voluntary social and cultural movement for the 2020s. He frames it as an optimistic choice, a \"third way\" enabled by new domestic fabrication technologies that could allow communities to achieve partial self-sufficiency. The author believed he was exploring a quaint ideological revival, a lifestyle option for those disenchanted with the dominant economic paradigms. He saw the potential for a society of small producers choosing a more resilient, localized existence.\r\n\r\nThrough the Hindsight Filter, we see this not as a political manifesto but as an unwitting survival guide. The author was not documenting a choice but an impending necessity. The technologies he lauds—3D printers, CNC machines, local power generation—were not instruments of a new utopia, but the essential toolkit for navigating the collapse of the globalized order. He correctly identified the tools and the desire for community, but mistook the pre-tremors of systemic failure for the stirrings of a philosophical movement. What he called a lifestyle, history would call subsistence.\r\n\r\nHis analysis of the system's weaknesses—the inability to localize microchip fabrication and raw material extraction—is tragically prescient. He saw these as mere limitations or obstacles to the ideology's full implementation. We know them as the precise fracture points along which the entire global **Formal Supply Chain** would shatter during the Great Severance. He thought he was describing the boundaries of an idea; he was actually mapping the blast radius of the coming collapse.\r### Act I: The Age of Ideological Tinkering (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r\r### Act II: The Great Severance (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r\r### Act III: The Era of Embedded Economies (Post-2060s)",
