      "body": "# The Purge of the Paper Citizens\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact is a legal decree masquerading as a sports scandal. The author is reporting on a human rights issue—the revocation of citizenship from a national hero. They see it as a \"crackdown\" by an authoritarian government, a violation of the norms of gratitude and civic belonging.\r\nFrom my perspective in 2100, we read this as a foundational precedent for the Great Contraction of the state. Kuwait was an early adopter of a brutal logic that would soon sweep the globe: the realization that in a world of shrinking resources and rising instability, \"citizenship\" is not a permanent right, but a revocable license based on absolute, singular loyalty. This was the end of the \"civic nation\" and the return of the \"blood tribe.\"\r\nAllow me to trace the full history of this contraction, from the revoked passports of the Gulf to the gated rosters of the Stacks.\r\nHere is the constellation of this history, viewed through the Hindsight Filter:\r### Act I: The End of the Universal Passport (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r\r### Act II: [[The Great Dissolution]] of the Civic Contract (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r\r### Act III: The Subscription to the Stack (Post-2060s)",
