      "body": "# The Permission Slip for the End\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThis artifact is a work of sociological seismology. The authors, researchers at a risk institute, believe they are conducting a survey on \"public perception.\" In reality, they are recording the tremors of a psychological earthquake that had already fractured the foundation of the West.\r\nThe concept of \"Statusquotastrophe\"—that the continuation of the present is more dangerous than its disruption—is the single most important psychological explainer for the speed of [[The Great Dissolution]]. It explains why, when the institutions began to crumble, so few people rushed to prop them up. The authors found that the American public had already mentally seceded from the 20th century.\r\nAllow me to trace the full history of this sentiment, from the survey data of 2026 to the constitutional realities of my time.\r\nHere is the constellation of this history, viewed through the Hindsight Filter:\r### Act I: The Psychological Secession (2020s-2030s)\r\r\r\r### Act II: The Rush for the Exits (2030s-2050s)\r\r\r\r### Act III: The Institutionalization of Adaptation (Post-2060s)",
