      "preview": "The Autopsy of the Fragile Web This artifact is a technical post-mortem of a localized grid failure, written in the calm, problem-solving prose of 20th-century engineering. The author, analyzing the 2025 Iberian blackout, believes he is documenting a solvable software and policy issue. He sees \"o...",
      "body": "### **The Autopsy of the Fragile Web**\n\n\nThis artifact is a technical post-mortem of a localized grid failure, written in the calm, problem-solving prose of 20th-century engineering. The author, analyzing the 2025 Iberian blackout, believes he is documenting a solvable software and policy issue. He sees \"oscillations,\" \"reactive power,\" and \"premature disconnections\" as bugs to be patched in the march toward a net-zero future.\n\nFrom my perspective in 2100, we read this document not as a fixable glitch, but as the first major tremor of the Great Fragmentation. The author believes he is looking at a technical failure; we know he is looking at an epistemological one. This blackout was the exact physical manifestation of the \"Energy Cannibalism\" predicted by your era's thermodynamic realists. It proved that a highly complex, decentralized, and algorithmically managed system is inherently fragile when it encounters the hard limits of physics.\n\nAllow me to trace the full history of this \"fragile web,\" from the cascading failures of your time to the hardened, closed loops of mine.\n\nHere is the constellation of this history, viewed through the Hindsight Filter:\n### Act I: The Illusion of Decentralized Control (2020s-2030s)",
