      "body": "### **The Wall at the End of the Bloodline**\n\n\nThis artifact is a document of profound, quiet biological finality. It reads as a scientific curiosity from a Japanese laboratory—a twenty-year experiment pushing the boundaries of cellular replication in mice. The researchers sought to discover if life could be sustained indefinitely through the sheer repetition of a single genetic code. They found that it cannot.\n\nFrom my perspective in 2100, we read this paper not merely as a zoological study, but as the foreshadowing of a bitter lesson your civilization was about to learn. The researchers in Yamanashi identified a boundary where the universe vetoes the arrogance of the exact copy. They proved that life, like intelligence, requires the costly, messy friction of exchange to stave off entropy.\n\nAllow me to trace the full, revised history of this \"dead end,\" from the laboratories of your time to the strict breeding protocols of mine.\n\nHere is the constellation of this history, viewed through the Hindsight Filter:\n### Act I: The Hubris of the Frictionless Copy (2020s-2030s)",
