      "preview": "--- | OpinionEDITORIALThis piece expresses the views of its author(s), separate from those of this publication. The Detroit News May 16, 2026, 7:23 p.m. ET View Comments<video disablepictureinpicture=\"\" elementtiming=\"ar-lead-video-exco\" id=\"excocontentvideo\" muted=\"\" old-src=\"https://www.gannett...",
      "body": "---\n\n| Opinion[EDITORIAL](https://archive.is/o/d7mqH/https://www.detroitnews.com/opinion/editorials/)This piece expresses the views of its author(s), separate from those of this publication.\n\nThe Detroit News\n\nMay 16, 2026, 7:23 p.m. ET\n\n[View Comments](https://archive.is/o/d7mqH/https://cm.detroitnews.com/comment/)<video disablepictureinpicture=\"\" elementtiming=\"ar-lead-video-exco\" id=\"__exco_content_video\" muted=\"\" old-src=\"https://www.gannett-cdn.com/authoring/videos/presto/renditions/89630876007/92124f0a-05da-44f2-9c8e-d0b1412595fd/1080p_30fps.mp4\" playsinline=\"\" poster=\"/d7mqH/c3fee14397be5d2806b8ad23feea83b5f0f10046.webp\" preload=\"auto\" src=\"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7\" style=\"min-height:100%;z-index:0;display:block;height:100%;left:0px;max-width:100%;object-fit:contain;position:relative;top:0px;width:100%;aspect-ratio:16 / 9;\" class=\"hoverZoomLink\"></video>\n\nKeep Watching\n\nMichigan school board fights help for vulnerable students\n\nKaitlyn Buss discusses State Board of Education's pushback against federal scholarship program\n\nMichigan’s State Board of Education has once again chosen ideology over students.\n\nIn a resolution passed this week, the board formally opposed efforts to have Michigan participate in the new federal Educational Choice for Children Act scholarship program — a voluntary initiative that would allow private donations to fund scholarships for K-12 students through federal tax credits.\n\nThe reaction from the board was revealing.\n\nRather than approaching the proposal with curiosity or even cautious openness, members immediately framed it as a threat to public education. It has been mischaracterized a voucher program and members are concerned it would drain resources from public schools.\n\nMore: [Our View: Michigan families deserve aid, choice in education](https://archive.is/o/d7mqH/https://www.detroitnews.com/story/opinion/editorials/2026/04/18/michigan-families-deserve-aid-choice-in-education-opinion/89656829007/)\n\nBut why shouldn’t Michigan at least try every available option to help children succeed? Especially now.\n\nMichigan’s academic performance remains among the nation’s worst. Reading and math scores continue to lag. Chronic absenteeism exploded after the pandemic. Parents increasingly express frustration with school environments, curriculum disputes and a system that too often appears more focused on institutional preservation than student outcomes.\n\nAnd yet when presented with a program that could potentially expand opportunities for lower- and middle-income families — funded not by Lansing tax hikes but through incentivized private donations — the board’s instinct was not exploration. It was opposition.\n\nThat says a great deal.\n\nMore: [Whitmer is right to wait on federal school choice plan | Opinion](https://archive.is/o/d7mqH/https://www.detroitnews.com/story/opinion/2026/04/29/gov-gretchen-whitmer-is-right-to-wait-on-federal-school-choice-plan-decision/89837440007/)\n\nThe Educational Choice for Children Act would allow states to opt into a federal tax-credit scholarship system where donors receive credits for contributing to scholarship-granting organizations. Those organizations would then help eligible students afford tuition, tutoring, special-needs services, homeschooling expenses and other educational options.\n\nImportantly, states are not forced to participate. Michigan can choose whether to opt in, which makes the board’s resolution even more absurd.\n\nWhat is the danger in allowing Michigan families access to additional educational opportunities? Why should bureaucrats in Lansing decide that struggling students must remain trapped in one system, regardless of whether it is meeting their needs?\n\nGet the Michigan Politics newsletter in your inbox.\n\nWashington and Lansing, red and blue, we've got your government covered.\n\nDelivery: Daily\n\nYour Email\n\nInvalid email address\n\nMore: [Michigan school board fights help for vulnerable students | Buss](https://archive.is/o/d7mqH/https://www.detroitnews.com/story/opinion/columnists/kaitlyn-buss/2026/04/15/michigan-state-board-education-school-choice-federal-tax-credit-one-big-beautiful-bill/89621658007/)\n\nThe board insists its opposition stems from protecting public schools. But public education exists to serve students — not the other way around. That distinction matters.\n\nToo often in Michigan, education debates have become centered on protecting institutions, employees and political interests rather than asking what arrangements best help individual children flourish.\n\nAnd the reality is that many families already exercise school choice every day — through private schools, charter schools, homeschooling or by moving to districts they perceive as better. Wealthier families have long possessed educational options. Programs like these merely attempt to extend some measure of flexibility to families without those advantages.\n\nMore: [Michigan State Board of Education shouldn't block school choice | Opinion](https://archive.is/o/d7mqH/https://www.detroitnews.com/story/opinion/2026/04/20/bussard-michigan-state-board-of-education-shouldnt-block-school-choice-opinion/89646089007/)\n\nThe board’s resolution also reflects a broader ideological rigidity that increasingly dominates education politics in Michigan.\n\nAny proposal involving parental choice is immediately labeled an attack on public education. There is little acknowledgment that many parents simply want alternatives because they feel the current system is failing their children academically, socially or morally.\n\nNor is there much humility from a public education establishment presiding over years of declining outcomes.\n\nMichigan should absolutely scrutinize the details of any federal program. Questions about accountability, implementation and oversight are legitimate. But reflexively opposing even the possibility of expanded educational opportunity before the state has fully debated it demonstrates a defensive posture that has become all too common among education officials.\n\nAt some point, leaders must decide whether their priority is protecting systems or empowering families.\n\nThe State Board of Education made its choice this week. Lawmakers and Gov. Gretchen Whitmer should make a different one.\n\n[View Comments](https://archive.is/o/d7mqH/https://cm.detroitnews.com/comment/)",
